.TH "UPTERM" "1" "Oct 2026" "Upterm 0.0.0+dev" "Upterm Manual"

.SH NAME
upterm-session-info - Show a session, given its name or session ID


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBupterm session info NAME|ID [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Show a session, given its name or session ID: its status, how guests join
it, who is connected, and what guests may do.

.PP
NAME|ID is the session's name (the one 'upterm host' printed, or --name
gave it) or its session ID; 'upterm session list' shows both. A name is
looked up before an ID.

.PP
A session that has ended still answers, from the record it left behind: how
it ended.

.PP
Output formats:
  -o json   JSON

.PP
Exits 4 when no session has the name, and 1 for any other failure.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for info

.PP
\fB--hide-client-ip\fP[=false]
	Hide guests' IP addresses from upterm's output. When it isn't set (by flag, environment or config file), they are hidden in CI only; --hide-client-ip=false shows them there too.

.PP
\fB-o\fP, \fB--output\fP=""
	Output format: json


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Enable debug logging (log file: /home/user/.local/state/upterm/upterm.log). A non-empty DEBUG turns it on too.


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # Show the session named build:
  upterm session info build

  # The same session, by its session ID:
  upterm session info d3k5fia7m4us73ehblug

  # As JSON:
  upterm session info build -o json
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBupterm-session(1)\fP


